{
  "dom_id": "step-t6647",
  "name": "Rays",
  "problem": "A **ray** is similar to a line but has a starting point and extends infinitely in one direction only. An example of a ray is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14129501/assets/750aef546281b34fef8e9aef61c1260894128138022e971d6c19dfd834cfa1e2.png)\n\nA ray that starts at the point $P$ and passes through a point $Q$ is expressed as \n\n$$\n\\overset{\\longrightarrow}{PQ}.\n$$\n\nWe use the \"$\\longrightarrow$\" symbol to denote that the line extends forever in **one** direction. Notice that the arrow's tail is above the starting point $P.$\n\nSimilar to lines, rays have no width but are infinite in length.",
  "worked_solution": "",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
